Aristotle: poetry, representation, and an organised whole (@greek-aristotle-poetics: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-plato-aristotle, #source @greek-source-poetics, #philosophy @greek-comparative-inquiry, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-comparative-inquiry, #philosophy @greek-plotinus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-plotinus, #philosophy @greek-schools-life, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-schools-life) { r_5PBR5GVV367JEK4PF94Q2YJKBE
**Read:** [Poetics](../sources/Poetics.lingua#r_188F7W37RPBA4RSDC6C373SN67), translated by S. H. Butcher. Begin with chapters I–VI, then VII–IX and XIII–XIV. Read the complete short work to examine how the account of tragedy relates to epic, plot, recognition, reversal, and character.

**Preparation:** distinguish a description of a work from an interpretation supported by its features. [Symposium](../plato/Symposium.lingua#r_5NMHFGWX7QGRJ65XQDFFEG9K8B) supplies a useful comparison about literary form and philosophy, rather than a required earlier course.

Ask what representation, or mimesis, is meant to explain. Follow the account of a plot as an organised whole and the distinction between recounting what happened and constructing an intelligible sequence. Examine how the translation renders disputed terms, including catharsis, before treating an English word as a settled definition.

**Practice:** choose a short story or familiar dramatic episode. Identify its beginning, development, reversal or recognition if present, and ending. Explain whether removing an event changes the organisation of the whole. Distinguish a formal observation from an evaluation of the work.

**Check:** reconstruct one claim about plot or representation, test it against the example, and explain the limits of applying an account of ancient tragedy to another genre. Support an interpretation through both the philosophical passage and the work being discussed.

**Continue:** [Plotinus on beauty](../schools/Plotinus.lingua#r_375WTA2HXW04F1FC4FVW20B9N9) or [a comparison of accounts of art](../questions/Comparative-investigation.lingua#r_20BK69Q76HJXAGZ5J1T097X29G). **Return:** [Plato and Aristotle](../Plato-and-Aristotle.lingua#r_6Z4HA576BYZD1JVJ66TJE0QQJE) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
